    "excerpt": "A reference for the heads of ayah that the six counting madhhabs disagree on, shown in their place in the mushaf.",
    "html": "<h1 id=\"section-1\">What this project is</h1>\n<p>Ayah counting (ʿadad al-āy) is the science of how many ayahs each surah has and where each one ends, always attributed to the scholar who transmitted it. Six madhhabs were transmitted through the great cities, and ayah numbering differs between them.</p>\n<p>This reference gathers the positions those madhhabs disagree on, shows each in its place in the mushaf, and names which madhhabs count it as a head of ayah.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-2\">What the disagreement is</h2>\n<p>The disagreement is about where an ayah ends. It is not an addition to the ayahs, nor an omission from them, nor any change of wording.</p>\n<p>Surat al-Ikhlas is four ayahs in First Madinan, Last Madinan, Basran and Kufan, and five in Makkan and Damascene. The question is whether \"lam yalid wa-lam yulad\" is one ayah or two.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-3\">How to read the site</h2>\n<ul><li><b>Mushaf</b> — choose a counting madhhab and a surah; ayahs are numbered by that madhhab and disputed heads are marked in place.</li><li><b>Ayah counts</b> — totals for the Qur’an and for each surah in every madhhab.</li><li><b>Search</b> — reach a specific position by the wording of the fāṣilah or by ayah number.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-4\">Scope</h2>\n<p>The subject is numbering and its boundaries. Differences between the qiraat in wording and in the rules of recitation are outside the project.</p>\n<p>The text displayed is Uthmani orthography following the riwayah of Hafs from ʿAsim. Choosing another counting madhhab changes the numbering only, never the wording.</p>",
