    "slug": "reviewer-guide",
    "title": "Reviewer guide",
    "group": "curation",
    "language": "en",
    "direction": "ltr",
    "alternateSlug": "reviewer-guide-ar",
    "excerpt": "What a reviewer checks at each position, how evidence is recorded, and what each documentation state means.",
    "html": "<h1 id=\"section-1\">Reviewer guide</h1>\n<p>Review applies to the claim and its evidence, not to the tables or the generated outputs. Those follow the source layer and are not reviewed on their own.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-2\">What to check</h2>\n<ul><li>The position of the fāṣilah and its wording as it stands in the text.</li><li>Its kind: the end of a Kufan ayah, or a break inside one.</li><li>The attribution: which madhhabs count it as a head of ayah, and which leave it.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-3\">Recording evidence</h2>\n<p>Each piece of evidence records the work, the locator within it, the edition relied on, the madhhabs it supports, and its tier.</p>\n<ul><li><b>primary</b> — an explicit statement in an accepted counting work.</li><li><b>commentary</b> — a commentary tied closely to a primary text.</li><li><b>secondary</b> — a later manual or specialist treatment.</li><li><b>modern-reference</b> — a contemporary reference used as context.</li><li><b>api-check</b> — a machine-readable external check, never sufficient alone.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-4\">Documentation states</h2>\n<ul><li><b>uncited</b> — no citation entered yet.</li><li><b>secondary_only</b> — supported only by commentary, later manuals, or modern references.</li><li><b>primary_cited</b> — at least one primary citation, no recorded review yet.</li><li><b>primary_cited_and_reviewed</b> — a primary citation and a recorded scholarly review.</li><li><b>disputed</b> — sources or reviewers disagree.</li><li><b>unresolved</b> — known to need more work; not to be treated as settled.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-5\">When sources conflict</h2>\n<p>Nothing is adjudicated without evidence and no disagreement is quietly smoothed away. The position stays marked as disputed until specialists settle it.</p>",
